// Package phonetic defines how words turn into sound-alike keys and which encoder serves which language.
//
// An Encoder appends one or more keys for a word to a caller's slice. Language packages register their encoder at init with Register, and callers resolve one with For, which accepts a language or locale code ("de", "de-DE", "en_GB") and falls back to English. Importing a language package is what makes it available, so a binary carries only the languages it imports.
//
// English (Double Metaphone), German (Kölner Phonetik), French and Spanish have encoders. Italian has none and falls back to English: on its first corpus (internal/core/match/testdata/phonetic/it.yaml) English Double Metaphone makes 56 of 57 decisions. The grown Italian corpus (internal/core/match/testdata/grown/it.yaml, 364 cases written blind) was meant to settle it, and it did not leave a gap for an encoder to close: English Double Metaphone makes 332 of 364 decisions (91.2%, Wilson 95% interval 87.9 to 93.7), and decides all 96 cases in the classes an Italian encoder would model (geminates, c and g before e and i, gn and gli, z and s, silent h, dropped accents). Of the 32 misses, 13 are number forms the normaliser does not parse (compound number words such as "millenovecentonovantadue" and "centoventisei", "otto e mezzo" for 8½), and the rest are real-word substitutions, splits, letter names and absent titles, which no spelling-to-sound rule reaches. No published Italian phonetic algorithm was found to adopt either: the Soundex and Metaphone families are built on English spelling, and Italian adaptations are ad hoc rule sets. An Italian encoder was therefore not built; revisit it if a corpus shows Italian misses that sound-alike keys would fix.
//
// Every encoder follows one contract: the input is already folded (lowercase, diacritics stripped as match.Normalize does); a word made only of ASCII digits is its own key; a word that encodes to nothing is its own key, so a non-empty word never gets an empty key; there are at most three keys, primary first, without duplicates; keys other than those fallbacks are ASCII; and the encoder is stateless and safe for concurrent use.
package phonetic
